{# ── Chapter: Hook ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="hook">
  <h2>A scroll handler that fires 200 times before you've scrolled an inch</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      You add a scroll listener to update a "back to top" button's visibility. It works, but scrolling the page suddenly feels sticky and laggy, especially on a trackpad or phone.
    </p>
    <p>
      The problem: the <code>scroll</code> event can fire dozens of times per second, and your handler runs every single time, even though the screen can only actually repaint about 60 times a second.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      Scroll events fire far more often than the browser can usefully paint. On a fast trackpad or a fling gesture on mobile, you can see 100+ scroll events in under a second, each one running your full handler.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      The fix is not debouncing, which delays the response entirely, it's throttling to the paint cycle using <code>requestAnimationFrame</code>, so your handler runs at most once per frame no matter how many scroll events fired in between.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

{# ── Chapter: Concept ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="concept">
  <h2>Coalescing scroll events to one update per frame</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      The trick: instead of doing work directly inside the scroll handler, just record that a scroll happened. Then, once per animation frame, check if a scroll happened and do the actual work then.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      The pattern uses a boolean flag and <code>requestAnimationFrame</code>: the scroll listener sets a "ticking" flag and schedules a frame; the frame callback does the real work and clears the flag, so a burst of scroll events between frames only produces one update.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      This differs from debounce/throttle utility functions with a fixed millisecond window: rAF-based throttling is tied directly to the browser's own paint cadence, so it self-adjusts to the display's actual refresh rate (60Hz, 120Hz, or whatever the device supports) instead of guessing a timing constant.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

{# ── Chapter: Story ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="story-1">
  <h2>The sticky header that made scrolling feel broken</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      A team added a sticky header that shrinks as you scroll down. The scroll handler recalculated header height and updated several CSS custom properties on every single scroll event.
    </p>
    <p>
      On a MacBook trackpad this looked fine, since trackpad scroll events are relatively spaced out. On a phone, the same handler firing on every touch-move event made scrolling visibly stutter.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      The fix took one afternoon: wrap the recalculation in the tick/rAF pattern, no other logic changed. The scroll handler itself still fires at native frequency, it just does almost nothing anymore, only setting a flag.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      The team also added a Long Animation Frame check in their RUM setup afterward, specifically watching for scroll-adjacent frames over 16ms, since that's the class of regression this fix targets and the class most likely to silently reappear if someone adds new work to the handler later.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

